  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/039c88a5cb4278b5522ccb95cde8a92f48ae572deb3ad38845c2ceaaec1e90ac.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?",
  "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only.",
